<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcantonio Colonna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1535">1535</a>–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Marcantonio II Colonna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcantonio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> was an Italian nobleman and commander of the Colonna family. He led the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> in the fleet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571, where the Ottoman navy was defeated, and was given a triumphal entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> as viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1577 until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcantonio-colonna.jpg" alt="Marcantonio Colonna" width="320" height="547" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scipione Pulzone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcantonio_II_Colonna_by_Scipione_Pulzone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marcantonio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> was born in 1535 at Civita Lavinia into the Colonna family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, then one of the most powerful feudal dynasties of the Papal States and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. He was the son of Ascanio Colonna, who served Holy Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. His mother, Giovanna d'Aragona, a niece of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a>, divorced Ascanio and took the boy with her when he was one year old, so he grew up as a political enemy of his father. In 1548 he became a page to Prince Philip, and in 1552 he married Felicia Orsini, daughter of Girolamo Orsini, Lord of Bracciano. His father disinherited him that December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1553 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> served in the Spanish army under Fernando Álvarez de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, and used Spanish force to seize his father's lands. He fought in the cavalry at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marciano">Battle of Marciano</a>, which destroyed the Franco-Sienese army. When the pro-French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Pope Paul IV</a> was elected, Colonna plotted a rebellion against him; the plot was discovered, and in May 1556 the pope excommunicated him and confiscated his lands. In the war that followed he captured Paliano, Palestrina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracina">Terracina</a>, defeated Giulio Orsini at Acuto in 1557 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in triumph. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a> lifted the excommunication in 1559.</p><p>In the summer of 1570 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> made him Captain General of the papal fleet, although he had no direct naval experience. His first campaign, to relieve the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, broke down in quarrels with Giovanni Andrea Doria, and a storm between Corfu and Ancona sank most of the papal flotilla. In 1571 he persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to stop negotiating with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> was signed on 25 May, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> as lieutenant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>. At Lepanto on 7 October 1571 he came to the aid of Don John's flagship, the Real, and was shot twice in the chest. The following year he faced the Ottoman admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali">Occhiali</a> in the Cerigo Channel, where Occhiali declined battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> died on 1 August 1584, in circumstances that are not recorded. As Lord of Marino he was honoured by its inhabitants with an annual celebration, which survives as the Sagra dell'Uva. His daughter Costanza Colonna was the first patroness of the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, and his son Ascanio Colonna became a cardinal and viceroy of Aragon. In November 2022 the Italian Navy launched an offshore patrol ship named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcantonio Colonna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Papal galleys under <b>Marcantonio Colonna</b> served at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, and the War of Castro of 1641–1644 showed the limits of papal arms against small Italian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lanuvio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinaceli">Medinaceli</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=1566" aria-label="Lanuvio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Marcantonio Colonna"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5805 3963h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5805 3963h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=1566">What was here in 1566? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
